nodecode-davinci-resolve is a rewrite of wassermanproductions's wassermanproductions/hermes-davinci-resolve-plugin:

https://github.com/wassermanproductions/hermes-davinci-resolve-plugin
commit bbd5b207f823f16a20e756bfa80f44ad34790a3f
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Sam Wasserman (LICENSE)

The rewrite keeps the plugin's two halves and its vocabulary. The seven tools
carry the same work as the original's fourteen: the live verbs (status,
import, marker, render) speak to the running Resolve through the same
DaVinciResolveScript module, and the interchange verbs (media scan, FCPXML
timeline, marker CSV) write the same artifacts, with the same FCPXML 1.10
shape, marker placement and CSV columns. The dry-run rule is the original's:
a mutation without confirm reports its plan and changes nothing. Left out: the
app launcher, the media-pool timeline builders (create, append, scripted rough
cut) and the capabilities listing, which the manual now carries; and ffprobe
duration probing, since the rewrite sizes a clip from one length for the whole
timeline. Everything that ran in Python is Common Lisp except the scripting
bridge itself, which is one small program the cell hands to the operator's own
interpreter, because Resolve publishes its API in Python and nowhere else.
